Intermittent, timing-dependent failure only observed under the full concurrent test suite (not reproducible in isolation): z3-4.14.1 emits an undocumented multi-line diagnostic comment block (e.g. "; :zzz line: 2 position: 0" followed by a "; Suppported get-info parameters:" listing) on stdout immediately after each "unsupported" response to an unrecognized get-info parameter, which jSMTLIB's response parser normally strips as standard SMT-LIB ';' comments; under concurrent solver-process load a chunk of one of these comment blocks occasionally leaks into the raw text handed to the next command's response parser instead of being fully consumed with the first, producing an unparseable "Could not parse response from the solver for: ..." error and shifting the real response down by one slot -- the same solver-process stream-reading race also seen with z3-4.14.1 on ok_setOptionalOptions.tst and z3-4.8.12 on err_declareSortParameter_sortAfter.tst, not a defect in the test or golden Confirmed by reading SolverProcess.StreamGobbler.run(): its read loop offers a chunk to the queue as soon as endsWith() sees a balanced/terminated buffer, decoupled from whether a consumer is currently waiting -- a stray comment line with no unbalanced parens satisfies that immediately on its own if it happens to arrive as a separate OS-level read after the real response was already finalized, so it gets queued as a standalone chunk that the NEXT listen() call then consumes first. A real fix would need stdout to debounce/settle similarly to how errorSettleMillis already does for stderr, at a latency cost paid on every command for every solver, not just the rare versions that emit this quirk -- not attempted without sign-off given that tradeoff.

See https://github.com/smtlib/jSMTLIB/issues/99.
